= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Ten questions in the CUET UG style. Attempt all of them — the paper gives $+5$ for each correct answer and takes nothing away for a wrong one.

#mcq(1, [Which of the three countries launched its economic reforms FIRST?], (
  [India in 1991], [China in 1978], [Pakistan in 1988], [all simultaneously],
))

#mcq(2, [India's recent economic growth has been led mainly by the:], (
  [manufacturing sector], [services sector], [agricultural sector], [mining sector],
))

#mcq(3, [China's growth has been powered chiefly by:], (
  [export-oriented manufacturing], [agricultural exports], [remittances], [foreign aid],
))

#mcq(4, [The one-child population policy was a feature of:], (
  [India], [Pakistan], [China], [Bangladesh],
))

#mcq(5, [Among India, China and Pakistan, the HIGHEST rank on life expectancy and literacy belongs to:], (
  [India], [Pakistan], [China], [all three equal],
))

#mcq(6, [Special Economic Zones (SEZs) as a reform tool were pioneered by:], (
  [India], [China], [Pakistan], [the WTO],
))

#mcq(7, [Pakistan's economy has traditionally leaned heavily on:], (
  [agriculture and remittances], [software exports], [heavy industry], [tourism],
))

#mcq(8, [A common starting point for India, China and Pakistan was that all three:], (
  [were high-income democracies], [began as low-income economies adopting planning], [had fully industrialised by 1950], [rejected the public sector],
))

#mcq(9, [India's reforms were SLOWER than China's mainly because India is a:], (
  [closed economy], [democracy needing debate and consensus], [poor country], [agricultural economy],
))

#mcq(10, [China's one-child policy has produced which demographic consequence today?], (
  [an unusually young population], [a rapidly ageing population], [massive immigration], [zero population decline concerns],
))

= Detailed Solutions

Each solution shows the working, not just the verdict — cover the answer line, attempt the reasoning, then compare.

#solution(1, [China's U-turn began in 1978 — decollectivising agriculture and opening SEZs — thirteen years before India's 1991 LPG and roughly a decade before Pakistan's IMF-backed adjustment.], answer: [B])

#solution(2, [India's famous growth story runs through services — IT, telecom, finance — while manufacturing skipped a stage and agriculture still holds too many workers.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [China became the factory of the world: export-led manufacturing absorbed workers, built supply chains and drove the fastest growth of the three.], answer: [A])

#solution(4, [The strict one-child policy belongs to China — introduced in the late 1970s to brake population growth, relaxed only after ageing concerns mounted.], answer: [C])

#solution(5, [Mass early investment in health and schooling puts China on top of the human-development ladder; India sits between, Pakistan trails on these indicators.], answer: [C])

#solution(6, [SEZs — concentrated zones with easier rules for export industry — were China's reform laboratory from 1978; India adopted the idea much later.], answer: [B])

#solution(7, [Pakistan's economy rests on agriculture, foreign aid and worker remittances — with a narrow, textile-heavy export base and a thin industrial leg.], answer: [A])

#solution(8, [The comparison's whole point: three low-income, agrarian economies each adopted planning and public sectors — then POLICY choices produced divergent outcomes.], answer: [B])

#solution(9, [A democracy must debate, persuade and build consensus — India's LPG arrived through negotiation and half-steps, while China's one-party state pushed reform top-down.], answer: [B])

#solution(10, [Braking births fast inverted China's age pyramid — today's China ages rapidly, with fewer workers supporting more elderly citizens.], answer: [B])

